// THE BROKER: SELECTION DECIDES WHICH CELL, THE STORE DECIDES WHETHER WE GET IT.
//
// This is the first production consumer of three authorities that have each
// been complete and unconsumed. product.fabric.selection's select_supply ranked
// nothing for anyone; gunbc.fabric_floor_dispatch turned the floor's own Work
// into a Demand and had only witness rows behind it; and the file-backed
// compare-and-set had no caller at all. The value of this cut is not new
// vocabulary -- it invents none -- it is that those three stop being furniture.
//
// THE INVARIANT THE WHOLE CONTROL PLANE RESTS ON, and the reason the two steps
// are in this order and not the other: SELECTION IS A DECISION, NOT A CLAIM.
// Ranking an offer highest does not reserve the cell it names. Two brokers
// ranking the same roster reach the SAME answer, so selection alone would hand
// one cell to both. The compare-and-set is what makes the reservation exclusive,
// and it is the store rather than a lock that decides, so it holds across hosts.
//
// Therefore: no committed compare-and-set, no reservation. A caller that reads
// CellReserved has a slot generation that only one writer can hold. A caller
// that reads anything else has nothing, and the arms below say precisely what
// kind of nothing, because "we did not get the cell" has at least four causes
// with four different remedies -- nobody was admissible, somebody beat us, the
// store could not answer, and the attempt was malformed before it was tried.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO. It does not issue an ExecutionGrant, start a process,
// or reach a host. A reservation is the precondition for a Grant and is not one:
// ExecutionGrant carries resource and money reservations that must commit
// atomically across ledgers, and manufacturing one here from a slot generation
// would fabricate the very atomicity that record exists to guarantee. The Grant
// cut consumes this outcome; it does not live in it.

// The one deployment authority that supplies the store's root parameter.
//
// The mechanism takes a root and the deployment names it: compare_and_set is
// parameterized so a second control plane is a different root and not a second
// implementation, while this function is the single place this control plane's
// root is stated. Hard-coding the root inside the store would fuse a policy
// fact into a mechanism, which is the layer inversion the interface warns about.
fn fabric_control_plane_store_root() -> NonEmptyStr {
  "/var/lib/gunbc/fabric/allocation"
}

// THE ALLOCATION KEY IS PROJECTED FROM THE CELL'S OWN IDENTITY, NEVER AUTHORED.
//
// runner_slot_instance_name is already the single authority for how a slot is
// spelled, so the key is that spelling and nothing else. Taking a NonEmptyStr
// here instead would make the filesystem layout a SECOND identity authority --
// two spellings of one cell, free to disagree, with the store silently
// arbitrating between them. A caller cannot name the slot it is about to
// occupy; it can only hand over the identity and be told where that lands.
//
// It is slash-free by construction (host + "-" + index), which is what the
// store's own key wall requires: a key carrying a separator is refused at the
// mint because it would escape the store root.
fn fabric_cell_allocation_key(slot: RunnerSlotIdentity) -> NonEmptyStr {
  runner_slot_instance_name(slot: slot) as NonEmptyStr
}

// What the reserved slot holds: the pair that says WHO holds this cell and FOR
// WHAT. Rendered rather than structured because the store is concrete at
// NonEmptyStr, and the digest is derived from this exact rendering so the
// payload and its content hash cannot disagree.
fn fabric_reservation_payload(
  demand: FabricIdentity<NonEmptyStr, DemandKey>,
  offer: FabricIdentity<NonEmptyStr, OfferKey>,
) -> NonEmptyStr {
  concat(concat(demand.key as String, "|"), offer.key as String) as NonEmptyStr
}

// EVERY WAY THIS ENDS, TYPED AND SEPARATE, because they have different remedies.
//
// NoCellAdmissible is a market answer: the roster was read and nothing in it met
// the demand, and it carries TWO populations because they are two different
// answers wearing one word. `considered` is the market's own rejection of the
// offers it was allowed to rank; `refused` is everything removed BEFORE ranking
// for crossing the capacity-class line, which the market never saw and can say
// nothing about. Both empty means nothing was offered at all.
//
// THE SECOND FIELD IS THE REPAIR OF A SILENT NARROW, NOT AN ADDITION. Admission
// filtered the roster and dropped its refusals on the floor, so a demand refused
// entirely on a safety boundary was indistinguishable from a demand nobody
// offered anything for -- opposite remedies, one symbol, and the annotation here
// claimed the refusals were counted while nothing counted them. ReservationLost is
// a race: our selection was fine and another writer committed the slot first, so
// the remedy is to re-observe and retry. StoreUnavailable is operational: the
// store could not answer, which says nothing about the cell. AttemptRefused is
// ours: the attempt did not survive the store's mint, which is a defect in this
// module rather than a condition in the world.
//
// CellSelectionTieUnresolved is NOT a market answer and is deliberately not folded
// into NoCellAdmissible: offers WERE admissible and the tie policy could not choose
// between two of them, so the remedy is to fix the policy or the offer identities
// rather than to conclude the fleet had nothing. Under the roster-order policy this
// selection is pinned to it cannot occur; the arm exists so that a later change of
// policy cannot make it disappear into a wrong one.
//
// Collapsing any pair of these into one arm would hand a caller a symbol it
// cannot choose a remedy from, which is the state-space conflation this corpus
// keeps finding.
// PermitNamesAnotherUseForCell IS THE DECIDING PATH'S OWN USE CHECK. It carries the STANDING
// rather than a demand pair: the permit can disagree about the offer, the route, the work or the
// demand, and an arm that reported two demand identities for a WORK mismatch would print two
// values that are equal beside a refusal - a fabricated plausible output in the sense DESIGN
// section 5 forbids, and one that would send a reader looking at the wrong field. The roster this broker
// ranks was bound at some earlier moment, for whatever demand and work the binder was serving
// then, and nothing in the selection fold re-reads that. So the candidate that WON is checked
// against the demand actually being served, and a mismatch refuses rather than reserving money and
// a slot for a use nobody admitted. Before this arm the check existed only in
// product.fabric.grant_issue's issue_grant, which this path never calls.
type CellReservation
  = CellReserved {
      offer: FabricIdentity<NonEmptyStr, OfferKey>,
      slot_key: NonEmptyStr,
      generation: Int,
      account: MoneyAccount,
    }
  | NoCellAdmissible {
      considered: List<OfferCandidacy<NonEmptyStr>>,
      refused: List<CapacityClassAdmission>,
    }
  | CellSelectionTieUnresolved {
      digest: Fnv1a64StructuralDigestHex,
      considered: List<OfferCandidacy<NonEmptyStr>>,
    }
  | BudgetRefused { slot_key: NonEmptyStr, refusal: MoneyRefusal }
  | ReservationLost { slot_key: NonEmptyStr }
  | PermitNamesAnotherUseForCell { standing: PermitStanding<NonEmptyStr> }
  | StoreUnavailable { slot_key: NonEmptyStr, cause: CasStoreFailure }
  | AttemptRefused { slot_key: NonEmptyStr, detail: NonEmptyStr }

// THE CELL'S HOLDER IS ITS RESERVATION, AND THE OWNER IS DERIVED FROM IT.
//
// The hold protocol asks for a holder value; the fabric's holder is the
// reservation reference, which is already the ONE identity the money encumbrance
// and the cell agree on. Deriving the owner here rather than rendering a second
// spelling keeps the slot payload byte-identical to what this module wrote before
// the protocol was extracted: "held|" followed by exactly the reservation
// reference. A slot written by the pre-extraction broker decodes under the shared
// codec to a hold owned by that same reservation.
fn fabric_hold_owner(reservation: ReservationRef) -> DurableHoldOwnerRef {
  (reservation as String) as DurableHoldOwnerRef
}

// The release reference is the reason, unchanged: "free|" followed by exactly the
// reason the caller gave, as before.
fn fabric_hold_release_ref(reason: NonEmptyStr) -> DurableHoldReleaseRef {
  (reason as String) as DurableHoldReleaseRef
}

// The reservation half, reached only once selection has named an offer.
//
// THERE IS NO EXPECTATION PARAMETER ANY MORE, AND ITS REMOVAL IS THE FIRST
// AMENDMENT OF THE EXTRACTION. This function used to take a CasExpectation from
// its caller -- absent for a first reservation, the generation a release left
// behind for a re-reservation -- and hand it to the store. CAS judged the
// generation and nothing judged occupancy, so a caller presenting the correct
// generation of a slot that was HELD at that generation overwrote the hold. The
// shared acquire reads the slot itself, admits only absent or free, and derives
// its expectation from what it read; a caller has no parameter through which to
// name a generation. A cell held by THIS reservation's own spelling is still
// occupied: the same spelling does not prove the earlier process is gone.
//
// The occupied and race-lost arms both map to ReservationLost, which is what a
// caller presenting ExpectSlotAbsent against a held slot received before: the
// remedy in both cases is to re-observe and try again once the holder releases.
// An undecodable slot maps to AttemptRefused, as the live probe already
// classified it: it is not a race and not a store fault, and nothing but an
// operator can move it.
fn reserve_selected_cell(
  root: NonEmptyStr,
  slot_key: NonEmptyStr,
  demand_id: FabricIdentity<NonEmptyStr, DemandKey>,
  offer_id: FabricIdentity<NonEmptyStr, OfferKey>,
  account: MoneyAccount,
) -> CellReservation {
  match file_hold_acquire(
    root: root,
    slot_key: slot_key,
    requested_owner: fabric_hold_owner(
      reservation: fabric_money_reservation_ref(demand: demand_id, offer: offer_id),
    ),
  ) {
    FileHoldAcquired { slot_key: _, owner: _, generation: g } =>
      CellReserved {
        offer: offer_id,
        slot_key: slot_key,
        generation: cas_generation_count(g: g),
        account: account,
      }
    FileHoldOccupied { slot_key: _, holder: _, generation: _ } => ReservationLost { slot_key: slot_key }
    FileHoldAcquireLost { slot_key: _ } => ReservationLost { slot_key: slot_key }
    FileHoldAcquireStoreUnavailable { slot_key: _, cause: c } => StoreUnavailable { slot_key: slot_key, cause: c }
    FileHoldSlotUndecodable { slot_key: _, generation: _, detail: d } => AttemptRefused { slot_key: slot_key, detail: d }
    FileHoldKeyNotSlotAddressable { slot_key: _ } =>
      AttemptRefused { slot_key: slot_key, detail: "allocation key is not slot-addressable" }
  }
}

// The whole transition, in the only order that is correct: rank, then reserve.
//
// Selection is consulted first because reserving before knowing which cell to
// reserve is not a cheaper ordering, it is a different operation. And no
// reservation is attempted on the NoAdmissibleOffer path -- a broker that
// reserved something anyway when nothing was admissible would be fabricating a
// decision the market refused to make.
// THE ARROW BETWEEN THE MARKET'S DECISION AND THE CELL IT LOCKS.
//
// An earlier cut of the broker took the slot as a parameter beside the
// selection, which let a caller reserve one cell against a decision that chose
// a different supplier's offer. Two true facts -- this offer won, lock that
// cell -- with nothing relating them: the reservation would be attributed to a
// decision that never made it, and every arm would still look plausible.
//
// The repair is construction rather than a check. A cell may enter the roster
// only through this mint, and the mint refuses unless the offer's executor is
// EXACTLY the slot's canonical instance name. So by the time selection ranks
// anything, every candidate already carries its cell, and a reservation for a
// cell the market did not choose has no spelling.
//
// THE RUNG IS PATH-SCOPED AND THE WEAKER PATH IS THE ONE THAT COUNTS. On the
// source-to-.dag acceptance path this is structurally impossible: sole_constructor
// confines construction and the validator here is fixed and module-owned, with
// zero caller freedom -- so it cannot be defeated the way a caller-supplied
// predicate can. On the EMISSION path it is unestablished: DESIGN carries an
// executed receipt that a fixed-law mint of exactly this shape emits as a pub
// struct with a pub field deriving Deserialize, and both a direct struct literal
// and a deserialization admit representatives from every refusal partition. The
// wall does not travel with the type. DESIGN takes a class's rung to be the
// minimum across its in-scope paths, so the honest statement is the pair, and
// the next-rung trigger is unforgeable construction surviving emission -- not
// anything this module can supply.
//
// WHAT THE MINT REFUSES THAT LOOKS LIKE IT SHOULD PASS, named so it is a
// deliberate refusal rather than a surprise: a HOST is not a cell. Fixtures in
// tree carry executor "srv3", and a slot renders as "srv3-06", so an offer
// executed by a host refuses here. That is correct -- a host runs many cells and
// reserving one generation against it would lock a name no single executor owns.
//
// Measured population behind the claim that this arrow is new: ten string-literal
// executors across dag/ and src/v2/ -- "gunbc" x5, "supplier" x2, "srv3" x2,
// "rented-pool" x1. Not one is a slot identity, so nothing in tree binds an offer
// to a CELL. An earlier statement of this said every fixture used "gunbc", which
// was generalized from one file rather than counted.
type FabricCellCandidate sole_constructor {
  slot: RunnerSlotIdentity
  candidate: CandidateOffer<NonEmptyStr>
}

type CellCandidateBinding
  = CellCandidateBound { bound: FabricCellCandidate }
  | OfferExecutorIsNotTheCell { slot_key: NonEmptyStr, executor: NonEmptyStr }

fn bind_fabric_cell_candidate(
  slot: RunnerSlotIdentity,
  candidate: CandidateOffer<NonEmptyStr>,
) -> CellCandidateBinding {
  let slot_key = fabric_cell_allocation_key(slot: slot)
  if (candidate.offer.executor as String) != (slot_key as String) {
    OfferExecutorIsNotTheCell { slot_key: slot_key, executor: candidate.offer.executor }
  } else {
    CellCandidateBound { bound: FabricCellCandidate { slot: slot, candidate: candidate } }
  }
}

// The winner's executor is the cell's canonical allocation key BY CONSTRUCTION,
// not by a second rendering: FabricCellCandidate cannot be minted unless the
// executor is exactly what fabric_cell_allocation_key produced for the slot it
// is paired with. Recovering the key from the selected offer is therefore the
// same renderer's output carried through selection, never a second identity
// authority -- which is the property the store's key must have.
fn selected_cell_allocation_key(offer: SupplierOffer<NonEmptyStr>) -> NonEmptyStr {
  offer.executor
}

fn fabric_cell_roster_candidates(roster: List<FabricCellCandidate>) -> List<CandidateOffer<NonEmptyStr>> {
  fold(roster, init: [], f: (acc, member) => acc + [member.candidate])
}

// ADMISSION RUNS BEFORE SELECTION, AND THE ORDER IS THE SAFETY PROPERTY.
//
// Fungibility asks whether an executor CAN satisfy the work. Once two offers are
// fungible they are interchangeable by definition, so a capacity-class boundary
// checked after ranking is a boundary already crossed. This removes unsanctioned
// executors from the roster before anything is ranked, so a control-plane cell
// is never a candidate for customer-executable demand and cannot be chosen by a
// tie-break, a price, or a later change to the ranking.
//
// ONE FOLD, BOTH HALVES, AND THE PREVIOUS SHAPE IS WHY. Admission used to be two
// folds over one roster -- one keeping the admitted through a Bool predicate,
// one collecting the refused -- and they could only agree by convention: each
// re-ran the admission judgement independently, so an edit to either could make
// a candidate appear in neither half or in both, and nothing would detect it.
// A partition is ONE decision with two outputs, so the halves cannot disagree
// (§2 -- the second traversal was a second representation of one judgement).
//
// The Bool predicate that selected the admitted half is deleted with it. It
// collapsed a refused capacity class and an unclassified executor into a single
// false, which are different problems -- one is an executor asking for work it
// is not sanctioned for, the other is an executor nobody has classified at all
// -- and an `if` over that Bool could name no remedy for either.
type CellAdmissionPartition
  = CellAdmissionPartition {
      admitted: List<FabricCellCandidate>,
      refused: List<CapacityClassAdmission>,
    }

fn partition_cell_roster(
  requirements: ExecutionRequirements,
  sanctions: List<ExecutorSanction>,
  roster: List<FabricCellCandidate>,
) -> CellAdmissionPartition {
  fold(
    roster,
    init: CellAdmissionPartition { admitted: [], refused: [] },
    f: (acc, member) =>
      match admit_executor_capacity(
        requirements: requirements,
        roster: sanctions,
        executor: member.candidate.offer.executor,
      ) {
        CapacityClassAdmitted { executor: _, class: _ } =>
          CellAdmissionPartition {
            admitted: acc.admitted + [member],
            refused: acc.refused,
          }
        CapacityClassRefused { executor: e, required: r, sanctioned: sd } =>
          CellAdmissionPartition {
            admitted: acc.admitted,
            refused: acc.refused + [CapacityClassRefused { executor: e, required: r, sanctioned: sd }],
          }
        ExecutorNotClassified { executor: e } =>
          CellAdmissionPartition {
            admitted: acc.admitted,
            refused: acc.refused + [ExecutorNotClassified { executor: e }],
          }
      },
  )
}

// ONE RESERVATION, ONE REFERENCE. The money encumbrance and the cell slot name
// the same reservation because they are derived from the same pair -- the demand
// and the offer -- through the very rendering the store slot holds. Minting a
// separate money key here would make the budget and the store disagree about
// what is being held, and nothing would ever detect it: two references that are
// both individually well-formed are indistinguishable from one reservation
// unless they are DERIVED from a common identity rather than agreed by
// convention (§3 -- a second name for one concept).
fn fabric_money_reservation_ref(
  demand: FabricIdentity<NonEmptyStr, DemandKey>,
  offer: FabricIdentity<NonEmptyStr, OfferKey>,
) -> ReservationRef {
  fabric_reservation_payload(demand: demand, offer: offer) as ReservationRef
}

// THE BUDGET REFUSAL RETURNS NEITHER A CELL NOR AN ACCOUNT, AND THAT -- NOT THE
// ORDER THESE TWO STEPS ARE WRITTEN IN -- IS THE SAFETY ARGUMENT.
//
// Two resources are held per reservation and they have opposite reversal costs.
// The budget is a PURE VALUE: reserving against it produces a new account and
// changes nothing anyone else can observe, so abandoning it costs exactly
// nothing -- the caller simply does not persist it. The cell is an EFFECT: a
// committed compare-and-set is visible to every other broker the instant it
// lands, and there is no arm here that un-commits it.
//
// SO THE GUARANTEE IS STATED OVER THE RETURNED VALUE, NOT OVER A SEQUENCE. On
// the BudgetRefused arm the store call is not part of the result, so it is
// never forced and no effect occurs; on every cell-refusal arm there is no
// account value in the caller's hands to persist. The partial commit -- an
// encumbrance without its cell, or a cell without its encumbrance -- has no
// constructor on any arm of CellReservation, so it is unwritable rather than
// checked (§4b, structurally impossible rather than validated).
//
// THIS WAS NEARLY DOCUMENTED AS AN ORDERING GUARANTEE AND IT IS NOT ONE. A
// witness asserting "the budget refuses before the store is touched" was
// authored, went green, and then STAYED GREEN under a mutation that moved the
// compare-and-set ahead of the encumbrance -- because in a lazy substrate the
// unreturned branch is never forced, so both spellings are equally safe and the
// row discriminated nothing. The executed receipt is what corrected the claim:
// the safety is carried by which arm carries what, and source order is free.
// Writing the encumbrance first is kept because it reads in the order a person
// reasons about it, and for no stronger reason than that.
//
// THE CURRENCY COMES FROM THE OFFER, NOT THE ACCOUNT. product.fabric.budget
// deliberately makes a request state its own currency so a mismatch is
// representable and therefore detectable; passing the account's own currency
// back to it would satisfy the check by construction and turn a real wall into
// a decoration that can never fire (§4b -- ask whether the RED is authorable).
// The supplier quotes in its currency and the account holds one, so the two can
// genuinely disagree, and a mutation substituting the account's currency was
// executed and turned the witness below red -- so the wall is load-bearing
// rather than merely present.
fn broker_reserve_for_demand(
  root: NonEmptyStr,
  demand: Demand<NonEmptyStr>,
  roster: List<FabricCellCandidate>,
  sanctions: List<ExecutorSanction>,
  account: MoneyAccount,
  maximum_buy_order: MoneyAmountMicro,
  delay_valuation: MoneyPerSecond,
) -> CellReservation {
  let partition = partition_cell_roster(
    requirements: floor_execution_requirements(),
    sanctions: sanctions,
    roster: roster,
  )
  match floor_supply_selection(
    candidates: fabric_cell_roster_candidates(roster: partition.admitted),
    maximum_buy_order: maximum_buy_order,
    delay_valuation: delay_valuation,
    affinity: (demand.id.key as String) as PlacementAffinityKey,
  ) {
    NoAdmissibleOffer { considered: c } =>
      NoCellAdmissible { considered: c, refused: partition.refused }
    EqualCostTieUnresolved { digest: d, cause: _, considered: c } =>
      CellSelectionTieUnresolved { digest: d, considered: c }
    OfferSelected { candidate: winner, cost: k, basis: _, horizon: _, considered: _, tie_policy: _ } =>
      match permit_standing(
        permit: winner.permit,
        offer: winner.offer.id,
        route: winner.offer.route,
        demand: demand.id,
        work: demand.work,
      ) {
      PermitNamesAnotherOfferThan { permit_names: n } =>
        PermitNamesAnotherUseForCell { standing: PermitNamesAnotherOfferThan { permit_names: n } }
      PermitNamesAnotherRouteThan =>
        PermitNamesAnotherUseForCell { standing: PermitNamesAnotherRouteThan }
      PermitNamesAnotherWorkThan { permit_work: pw, subject_work: sw } =>
        PermitNamesAnotherUseForCell {
          standing: PermitNamesAnotherWorkThan { permit_work: pw, subject_work: sw } }
      PermitNamesAnotherDemandThan { permit_demand: pd, subject_demand: sd } =>
        PermitNamesAnotherUseForCell {
          standing: PermitNamesAnotherDemandThan { permit_demand: pd, subject_demand: sd } }
      PermitAuthorizesUse =>
        reserve_priced_cell(
          root: root,
          demand_id: demand.id,
          offer: winner.offer,
          cost: k,
          account: account,
        )
      }
  }
}

// The priced half, split out so the money reference and the slot key are named
// once each rather than recomputed inside a match arm.
//
// The lease generation is 0 because this is a MINT: the fence in
// product.fabric.budget exists so that a LATER settle or release must present
// the generation it observed, and a first encumbrance has no prior generation
// to present. Deriving it from the CAS generation is not available and would be
// wrong if it were -- the money reservation is taken before the store is
// touched, so no CAS generation exists yet, and borrowing one would make the
// budget's fence depend on a fact from the other resource's clock.
fn reserve_priced_cell(
  root: NonEmptyStr,
  demand_id: FabricIdentity<NonEmptyStr, DemandKey>,
  offer: SupplierOffer<NonEmptyStr>,
  cost: SelectionCost,
  account: MoneyAccount,
) -> CellReservation {
  let slot_key = selected_cell_allocation_key(offer: offer)
  let reference = fabric_money_reservation_ref(demand: demand_id, offer: offer.id)
  match reserve_money(
    a: account,
    request: MoneyReservationRequest {
      reference: reference,
      generation: 0,
      maximum_liability: selection_liability(c: cost),
      currency: offer_quote_currency(q: offer.quote),
    },
  ) {
    MoneyRefused { refusal: r } => BudgetRefused { slot_key: slot_key, refusal: r }
    AccountAdvanced { account: advanced } =>
      reserve_selected_cell(
        root: root,
        slot_key: slot_key,
        demand_id: demand_id,
        offer_id: offer.id,
        account: advanced,
      )
  }
}

// WHAT THE SLOT SAYS ABOUT ITSELF NOW LIVES IN THE SHARED HOLD PROTOCOL.
//
// Until release existed the slot payload was write-only; release was the first
// reader, and it needed an answer the payload could not give -- IS THIS CELL
// HELD, AND BY WHICH RESERVATION. The typed held/free state, its refusing
// decode and the held|/free| spelling were first written here to answer that.
// They are now std.durable_exclusive_hold (the states and transitions) and
// gunbc.durable_exclusive_hold_file_store (the one codec and the CAS
// realization), because a second consumer needed the same protocol over a slot
// that is not a cell, and a copy would have been a second authority (DESIGN 3).
// Nothing of the old vocabulary survives here as a compatibility surface; the
// bytes on disk are unchanged and decode under the shared codec.

// EVERY WAY A RELEASE ENDS, and the separation is the same discipline the
// reservation carries: a caller must be able to choose a remedy from the symbol
// it gets back.
//
// HoldEndSlotNotHeld is the one that does not exist on the reservation side and
// is the reason this type is not CellReservation reused. It says the store was
// read and the cell is NOT held by the reservation being released -- already
// free, held by someone else, or carrying a payload this module cannot read.
// Collapsing it into a store failure would tell an operator to look at the
// filesystem when the actual answer is that their idea of who holds this cell
// is wrong.
type CellHoldEnd
  = CellHoldEnded {
      slot_key: NonEmptyStr,
      generation: Int,
      account: MoneyAccount,
    }
  | HoldEndBudgetRefused { slot_key: NonEmptyStr, refusal: MoneyRefusal }
  | HoldEndSlotNotHeld { slot_key: NonEmptyStr, detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | HoldEndLost { slot_key: NonEmptyStr }
  | HoldEndStoreUnavailable { slot_key: NonEmptyStr, cause: CasStoreFailure }
  | HoldEndAttemptRefused { slot_key: NonEmptyStr, detail: NonEmptyStr }

// THE PARTIAL RELEASE HAS NO CONSTRUCTOR EITHER, AND IT LEAKS THE OPPOSITE WAY.
//
// The reservation's partial commit leaks a hold nobody is accounting for. The
// release's partial commit leaks in both directions and they are not the same
// harm: freeing the cell while the encumbrance stands bills a customer for a
// machine that is idle, and releasing the money while the cell stays held
// strands capacity under a work nothing requires -- which is precisely the leak
// product.fabric.arbitration's ReleaseDirective exists to close, and it would
// be reopened here at the actuator if the two halves could come apart.
//
// So the same construction argument applies unchanged, for the same reason: the
// money release is a PURE transition producing a new account, and the slot
// write is the only effect. No arm of CellHoldEnd carries an account without a
// freed cell, and none carries a freed cell without an account, so neither half
// can be persisted alone. Source order is again free and is again not the
// guarantee -- that was established by mutation on the reservation path and the
// same laziness holds here.
//
// RELEASE IS ASSESS-THEN-COMMIT, and the fabric is the consumer whose settlement
// sits between the two: the shared protocol assesses the live observation --
// held, by THIS reservation, at the generation it was ACQUIRED -- and mints a
// release plan only from that observation; the money transition runs; and the
// plan is committed only on AccountAdvanced. The acquisition generation is the
// SECOND AMENDMENT of the extraction: the release used to present the observed
// generation and was fenced against a stale release only by the money ledger's
// lease generation beside it. The hold now fences itself, so a generation-1
// holder resuming after a later generation-3 reservation of the same spelling
// refuses at the slot and never reaches the ledger.
//
// The refusals of the protocol all map to HoldEndSlotNotHeld, whose meaning is
// unchanged: the store was read and the cell is NOT held by the hold being
// released -- absent, already free, another reservation's, this reservation's at
// another generation, or a payload nobody can read. The detail names which.
//
// THE OBSERVATION IS READ HERE, NEVER ACCEPTED. An earlier cut took a
// CasSlotObservation so that a witness could supply one; that made this the
// second release route beside release_reserved_cell_at, and a synthetic "held
// by this reservation" would have minted a plan that frees another
// reservation's real hold. The shared assessment performs the read itself, so
// the fabric's release walls are now evidenced on a real store (the wet fabric
// witness) and the protocol's walls on supplied values one layer down.
fn end_cell_hold(
  root: NonEmptyStr,
  slot_key: NonEmptyStr,
  reservation: ReservationRef,
  acquired_generation: Int,
  money: MoneyOutcome,
  reason: NonEmptyStr,
) -> CellHoldEnd {
  match file_hold_release_assess(
    root: root,
    slot_key: slot_key,
    owner: fabric_hold_owner(reservation: reservation),
    acquired_generation: acquired_generation,
    released_by: fabric_hold_release_ref(reason: reason),
  ) {
    FileHoldReleaseNotEligible { slot_key: _, refusal: r } =>
      match r {
        HoldReleaseObservationUnavailable { cause: c } =>
          HoldEndStoreUnavailable { slot_key: slot_key, cause: CasSlotObservationRefused { cause: c } }
        HoldReleaseSlotAbsent =>
          HoldEndSlotNotHeld {
            slot_key: slot_key,
            detail: "the slot is absent, so this cell is held by no reservation",
          }
        HoldReleaseAlreadyFree { generation: _, released_by: _ } =>
          HoldEndSlotNotHeld {
            slot_key: slot_key,
            detail: "the slot is already free, so there is no hold to release",
          }
        HoldReleaseHeldByAnotherOwner { generation: _, holder: holder } =>
          HoldEndSlotNotHeld {
            slot_key: slot_key,
            detail: concat("the cell is held by another reservation: ", holder as String) as NonEmptyStr,
          }
        HoldReleaseAgainstAnotherGeneration { acquired: a, observed: o } =>
          HoldEndSlotNotHeld {
            slot_key: slot_key,
            detail: concat(
              concat(
                concat("the cell is held by this reservation at generation ", to_string(cas_generation_count(g: o))),
                ", not the generation this release was acquired at: ",
              ),
              to_string(cas_generation_count(g: a)),
            ) as NonEmptyStr,
          }
        HoldReleaseSlotUndecodable { generation: _, detail: d } =>
          HoldEndSlotNotHeld { slot_key: slot_key, detail: d }
      }
    FileHoldReleasePlanned { plan: plan } => commit_hold_end(plan: plan, money: money)
  }
}

// The half that runs once the store has confirmed THIS reservation holds the
// cell at the generation it acquired. The plan carries the generation the store
// just reported rather than one the caller stated: a caller-supplied generation
// would be a second account of the slot's version and could name a moment the
// observation never saw -- which is why the plan has no constructor outside the
// store module.
//
// THE MONEY OUTCOME ARRIVES ALREADY COMPUTED AND THAT IS NOT A CALLER DECIDING
// IT. Each ending owns its own money transition -- release derives the amount
// from the ledger entry, settlement presents an actual spend -- and both are
// fenced on the lease generation inside product.fabric.budget. What this
// function decides is the only thing both share: an advanced account frees the
// cell, a refusal does not. Passing the outcome rather than re-deciding it here
// is what keeps ONE money authority; a match on which ending this is would be a
// second one, and it would have to be kept in step with budget.dag forever.
//
// It is also effect-free to pass: the substrate is lazy, so an unreached money
// transition is never forced, and the refusal arms above return before this
// function is entered at all.
fn commit_hold_end(plan: FileHoldReleasePlan, money: MoneyOutcome) -> CellHoldEnd {
  match money {
    MoneyRefused { refusal: f } => HoldEndBudgetRefused { slot_key: plan.slot_key, refusal: f }
    AccountAdvanced { account: advanced } => free_cell_slot(plan: plan, account: advanced)
  }
}

// The commit still tells a lost race from an unavailable store: a plan assessed
// at generation N and committed after the slot moved is HoldEndLost, which
// tells the caller its settlement and the slot have come apart, and a store
// fault would not tell it that.
fn free_cell_slot(plan: FileHoldReleasePlan, account: MoneyAccount) -> CellHoldEnd {
  match file_hold_release_commit(plan: plan) {
    FileHoldReleased { slot_key: k, generation: g } =>
      CellHoldEnded { slot_key: k, generation: cas_generation_count(g: g), account: account }
    FileHoldReleaseLost { slot_key: k } => HoldEndLost { slot_key: k }
    FileHoldReleaseStoreUnavailable { slot_key: k, cause: c } => HoldEndStoreUnavailable { slot_key: k, cause: c }
    FileHoldReleaseKeyNotSlotAddressable { slot_key: k } =>
      HoldEndAttemptRefused { slot_key: k, detail: "allocation key is not slot-addressable" }
  }
}

// THE TWO ENDINGS, AND WHY THEY ARE ONE OUTCOME TYPE RATHER THAN TWO.
//
// A hold ends abandoned or it ends consumed. Both leave the SAME state -- the
// cell free and the encumbrance closed -- so a second outcome vocabulary for
// settlement would be five refusal arms renamed, which is the fork DESIGN
// section 3 forbids and which drifts the moment one of them gains a cause the
// other lacks. What differs is the money transition and the evidence that
// authorizes it, and that difference lives in the two entry points below rather
// than in the answer they return.
//
// A caller does not need the outcome to tell it which ending happened, because
// it called one of them.
//
// The observation is READ rather than accepted from the caller, for the reason
// the store's own compare-and-set gives about attempts: a caller that supplies
// the observation is the author of the fact it is supposed to be reading, and
// nothing then relates that fact to the slot being ended. There is no longer a
// form that accepts one at any layer.
fn release_reserved_cell_at(
  root: NonEmptyStr,
  slot_key: NonEmptyStr,
  acquired_generation: Int,
  lease: LeaseIdentity,
  reservation: ReservationRef,
  reason: NonEmptyStr,
  released_at: Timestamp,
  account: MoneyAccount,
) -> CellHoldEnd {
  end_cell_hold(
    root: root,
    slot_key: slot_key,
    reservation: reservation,
    acquired_generation: acquired_generation,
    money: release_money(
      a: account,
      reference: reservation,
      generation: lease.generation,
      reason: reason,
      released_at: released_at,
    ),
    reason: reason,
  )
}

// SETTLEMENT IS NOT RELEASE WITH AN AMOUNT ATTACHED, AND THE RECEIPT IS THE
// DIFFERENCE. Release needs no evidence beyond the lease: abandoning a hold
// spends nothing, and the released quantity is whatever the ledger already
// says. Settlement CHARGES, so it needs something that establishes what was
// actually spent and that the spender was entitled to say so -- which is
// exactly what product.fabric.execution's receipt admission decides.
//
// So the admission runs FIRST and its refusal is typed rather than folded into
// the budget arm. A receipt from a different attempt, a different lease, an
// incomparable fingerprint and a stale generation are four different problems
// with four different remedies, and product.fabric.execution already separates
// them; collapsing them here into "the budget refused" would discard that work
// one layer down and tell an operator to look at an account when the actual
// answer is that the receipt belongs to something else.
//
// THE AMOUNT COMES FROM THE RECEIPT AND FROM NOWHERE ELSE. A settlement whose
// amount was a parameter beside the receipt would let a caller charge one
// figure while presenting evidence for another, and the two would be free to
// disagree with nothing able to detect it. Only a SettlementReceipt carries an
// actual spend, which is why the payload is matched rather than trusted: an
// execution receipt records billable seconds, which is an OBSERVATION and not
// authoritative spend -- product.fabric.execution says so in line, because the
// billing rule is the supplier's and elapsed time is not the charge.
type CellSettlementAdmission
  = SettlementAdmitted {
      reservation: ReservationRef,
      actual_spend: MoneyAmountMicro,
      settled_at: Timestamp,
    }
  | SettlementReceiptRefused { admission: ReceiptAdmission }
  | SettlementReceiptCarriesNoSpend { detail: NonEmptyStr }

// THREE GAPS THAT ARE ONE ABSENCE: NOTHING CONSTRUCTS AN ExecutionGrant.
//
// This function CONSUMES a grant, which is why the note lives here. Measured on
// origin/main, 2026-08-28: `ExecutionGrant {` appears at three sites and all
// three are test fixtures. Every production reference takes a grant as a
// parameter or filters a list of them. There is no producer.
//
// Read as three separate gaps a reader will do three pieces of work; they are
// one absence seen from three sides, and whoever writes the producer inherits
// all of it:
//
// ONE -- a ReleaseDirective cannot name a cell. product.fabric.arbitration
// produces the directive and nothing consumes it. It carries
// resource_reservations as ReservationRefs; the release actuator here needs a
// SLOT KEY. Nothing relates the two, and the fixtures are opaque literals
// ("reservation-cpu-1", "res-cpu-1") because no consumer has ever had to
// interpret one. The relation is not inventable safely: ReservationRef is a
// branded NonEmptyStr, so ANY string inhabits it and a parse has no shape to
// validate against -- a wrong guess is undetectable at the type level and
// releases a cell someone else holds.
//
// TWO -- the acquire-side atomic join is a REQUIREMENT with no implementation.
// product.fabric.execution states that every reservation is applied tentatively
// and committed only if EVERY ledger admits, and that a partial commit is
// unreachable only if the join is atomic rather than sequential. With no grant
// producer that has never been executed.
//
// THREE -- the identity layer is unproduced. Measured the same day over dag/,
// excluding fixtures and the brand declarations: ObservationReceiptRef,
// ExecutionAttemptKey, LeaseKey, GrantKey and ReceiptKey have ZERO construction
// sites. ReservationRef has two and both are in this module. So the producer
// does not merely assemble a grant; it decides five identity conventions that
// nothing decides today.
//
// THE DECIDED DESIGN, so the producer's author inherits a ruling rather than
// reopening it (operator ruling relayed 2026-08-28): when a directive CAN name
// a cell, refuse more than one resource reservation per directive as a typed
// located refusal, and fold over the atomic one-cell release. Do not build the
// N-cell reconciliation. The plural the model proves load-bearing is a list of
// DIRECTIVES -- a Work under re-execution holds two grants and two sets of
// reservations -- and that folds cleanly, leaving whole grants released and
// whole grants held with no grant in a partial state. Two-phase commit,
// compensating release and undo logs are all ruled out: they make the partial
// state reachable and then work to escape it.
//
// CITATION TREE, because this note will be read from main and half of it does
// not resolve there: fabric_reservation_payload and this module do not exist on
// main -- they are unmerged. On main NEITHER reference is derivable. On this
// stack the money reference has a modeled derivation and the resource reference
// still does not, which is the precise gap: the producer owns one derivation,
// not two.
fn admit_cell_settlement<P>(
  receipt: Receipt<P>,
  grant: ExecutionGrant<P>,
) -> CellSettlementAdmission {
  match admit_receipt_for_grant(r: receipt, g: grant) {
    ReceiptFromDifferentAttempt =>
      SettlementReceiptRefused { admission: ReceiptFromDifferentAttempt }
    ReceiptFromDifferentLease =>
      SettlementReceiptRefused { admission: ReceiptFromDifferentLease }
    ReceiptFingerprintIncomparable =>
      SettlementReceiptRefused { admission: ReceiptFingerprintIncomparable }
    ReceiptGenerationStale { presented: p, current: c } =>
      SettlementReceiptRefused { admission: ReceiptGenerationStale { presented: p, current: c } }
    ReceiptAdmissible =>
      match receipt.payload {
        SettlementReceipt { reservation: r, actual_spend: a, settled_at: t } =>
          SettlementAdmitted { reservation: r, actual_spend: a, settled_at: t }
        ExecutionReceipt { step_id: _, outputs: _, billable: _, completed_at: _ } =>
          SettlementReceiptCarriesNoSpend {
            detail: "an execution receipt records billable seconds, which is an observation rather than authoritative spend",
          }
        MaterializationReceipt { source: _, verified_at: _ } =>
          SettlementReceiptCarriesNoSpend {
            detail: "a materialization receipt establishes an artifact, not a charge",
          }
      }
  }
}

// The settlement outcome adds ONE arm to the shared ending vocabulary rather
// than restating it: the receipt can be refused before any hold is touched, and
// that is a state release genuinely cannot reach.
type CellSettlement
  = SettlementEnded { ending: CellHoldEnd }
  | SettlementNotAdmitted { slot_key: NonEmptyStr, admission: CellSettlementAdmission }

fn settle_reserved_cell_at<P>(
  root: NonEmptyStr,
  slot_key: NonEmptyStr,
  acquired_generation: Int,
  receipt: Receipt<P>,
  grant: ExecutionGrant<P>,
  reason: NonEmptyStr,
  account: MoneyAccount,
) -> CellSettlement {
  match admit_cell_settlement(receipt: receipt, grant: grant) {
    SettlementReceiptRefused { admission: a } =>
      SettlementNotAdmitted {
        slot_key: slot_key,
        admission: SettlementReceiptRefused { admission: a },
      }
    SettlementReceiptCarriesNoSpend { detail: d } =>
      SettlementNotAdmitted {
        slot_key: slot_key,
        admission: SettlementReceiptCarriesNoSpend { detail: d },
      }
    SettlementAdmitted { reservation: r, actual_spend: spend, settled_at: t } =>
      SettlementEnded {
        ending: end_cell_hold(
          root: root,
          slot_key: slot_key,
          reservation: r,
          acquired_generation: acquired_generation,
          money: settle_money(
            a: account,
            request: MoneySettlementRequest {
              reference: r,
              generation: receipt.lease.generation,
              actual_spend: spend,
              currency: account.currency,
              settled_at: t,
            },
          ),
          reason: reason,
        ),
      }
  }
}
